add("CritMistakeFix", "critic", f"Attack the round's mined-mistake plan. Primary mistakes: round-73 routed defects (late seat writes after push; PASS only in seat reports; counter survivors; scenario-count drift) — see {LANE}/corpus/lessons/round-73-verifier-spotcheck.md section 6 and {LANE}/.ufo/waves/round-73/w2/. Determine by evidence: which exact criterion or rule should have caught each (read QUALITY 17, 19, 23 and the protocol snapshot clause under /home/vgpnk/Projects/kimi-shokunin/skills/shokunin/protocol/PROTOCOL.md), whether the planned fix would FAIL on a recurrence (construct the recurrence for each: e.g. a seat writing into the tree after the freeze commit — which leg turns red? a counter reading config from the graded tree — which control?), and which fixes are not shown able to fail (CORPUS 'A fix is shown able to fail before it counts'). Give the minimal red-before for each, as a command a reviewer can run.")
add("CritFrozenCfg", "critic", f"Attack hardening H1 (counter reads constants from the claim file at a named FREEZE commit that is a strict ancestor of the lands revision). Read {LANE}/reports/round-73/accept-c.py, accept-ca.py and the wave-2 mutation findings. Construct the attacks that survive H1: (a) a freeze commit that is an ancestor of lands but whose claims.json was itself edited after a seat scored (how would a closure know? is the freeze hash pinned anywhere a lander cannot move — e.g. recorded in the fleet report, the LOG, the SCORECARD row?); (b) lands containing the freeze as a merge parent; (c) a freeze that is an ancestor but on a different branch tip; (d) counter source itself changed after freeze (its sha256 is pinned in claims.json? chicken-and-egg: the counter sha cannot be inside the file it is pinned by — propose the pinning scheme: pin in the freeze commit message / a sidecar committed in the same freeze commit). Give the exact scheme and the red-before commands.")
add("CritQuality25", "critic", f"Attack the QUALITY 25 plan (counter reads frozen config; junk-landing controls ship with the freeze). Read {LANE}/corpus/QUALITY.md criteria 9, 11, 17, 19, 23 and {LANE}/corpus/lessons/round-73.md. Construct three real or synthetic 'counter descriptions' in lesson form: (a) a counter reading its row set from the graded tree, (b) one reading from the freeze commit but shipping no junk controls, (c) one with both. State what a grep-based red-before check must match to fail (a) and (b) and pass (c); test the regex with `grep -E` on the three texts and paste the output. Find the smallest wording that cannot be satisfied by writing 'reads from freeze' without a git-show-of-freeze command in the counter source, and propose the verification one-liner a reviewer can run on any later counter.")
add("MutRegistryCollision", "mutation-tester", f"Mutation-test the id-collision guard (CORPUS item 10). Build a scratch fixture in D=$(mktemp -d) (git -C \"$D\" init -q; every git call names -C \"$D\" — or avoid git entirely) containing a registry copy of {LANE}/corpus/SOURCES.md and the staged {STAGE}/SOURCES.append.md; plant these mutations in the registry copy one at a time: (m1) a row `  | S883 | ...` indented 2 spaces; (m2) 3 spaces; (m3) 4 spaces (code block — must NOT collide per census rules); (m4) `|S883|` no spaces; (m5) tab-indented; (m6) lowercase s883; (m7) an id with 4+ digits `| S1005 |`. For each run the CORPUS item-10 command pair and report whether `collisions` is non-empty, and compare with `{FLEET}/scripts/sources-census.sh validate --file <fixture>` (does the census itself raise E03 for the duplicate?). Note: the LANE ALREADY collides with the staged ids S883/S884/S899/S900 (different documents) — so also test that the guard's non-empty collisions are expected here and that the by-document map, not collisions==empty, is the acceptance. Report a table m1..m7 with pass/fail and the guard's weaknesses.")
add("SentinelScope", "sentinel", f"Scope and safety review of the round's write surface before any landing. Check: (1) the staged entries and the four staged rows ({STAGE}/SOURCES.append.md rows S883, S884, S899, S900) and the two staged entry files for credential material, private URLs, `op://` refs, session tokens, tracking query strings; (2) the four source URLs are public ordinary-means pages (QUALITY 10) — any login walls, any region/consent walls, any hidden redirects to private hosts; (3) the draft claims' write paths stay inside {LANE} and {FLEET}/.ufo/reports/round-74/kotodama.md, never MANIFEST.json, {FLEET}/scripts, {FLEET}/templates, .ufo/state; (4) whether the content of the entries is a canon decision or a rights/attribution ruling (PROVINCE 'Refuses'): the entries describe how real practitioners specify settings and decide nothing about any world or reference work; (5) PROVINCE fit: is 'how fictional settings are specified' linguistic-cultural worldbuilding or lore's world-bible craft — give a verdict with path:line from PROVINCE.md and lore's PROVINCE.md ({FLEET}/lore-shokunin/PROVINCE.md if present).")
add("SimplifierRows", "simplifier", f"Minimality attack on claim C's row set. Using the four tentative rows S881..S884 and the two entries in {LANE}/reports/round-74/claims.json, decide the MINIMAL honest row set: for each row say whether a drafted entry sentence needs it (open {WA}/drafts/ if the scribes have written them; otherwise read the staged entries) and whether dropping it fails a claim-C leg (ROWS, REPOINTED, CITEDLINES, census W04/E03, QUALITY 15 one-id-per-line) or QUALITY 1 (provenance). Also test the opposite: is any cited-in-entry source missing a row (e.g. staged prose citing round-2/3 ids S742/S743/S754 that are NOT in the package set). Name cuts to the entries (sentences resting on a row you would not append) and the exact kill-rule consequence. Report keep/cut per row with the evidence.")

